{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792365042,"nonce":"k6JkUO4VJp0zGjzlX3FEd5Mnf1A=","message":"Blur cost is down in 027f141b (local): weights from the CPU, a blur wider than 8 px taken on the content shrunk by powers of two, scratch textures and the uniform buffer reused, and every pass confined to the layer's bounds (the producer's bounds already hold the content plus three deviations). Error against a CPU Gaussian: 2 of 255 up to sigma 8, 3 to 5 beyond (sigma 12, 16, 32 measured). Measured on this Mac (Metal), ms per call amortised over batches of 10, two runs agreeing. Whole 1920x1080 layer, gaussian vs kawase: sigma 1: 0.67-0.84 vs 0.42-0.46; 2: 0.84-1.28 vs 0.43-0.57; 4: 1.28-1.30 vs 0.41-0.60; 8: 2.3 vs 0.48-0.82; 16: 1.45 vs 0.86-1.97; 32: 1.32-1.34 vs 1.03. An element of 400x300 on that target (gaussian confined to its region, kawase on a tight texture): sigma 1: 0.21 vs 0.23-0.25; 2: 0.21-0.23 vs 0.22-0.23; 4: 0.20-0.21 vs 0.23; 8: 0.36-0.37 vs 0.27-1.16; 16: 0.22-0.62 vs 0.51-0.71; 32: 0.37-0.40 vs 0.52. So an ordinary layer costs what Kawase does; only a full-screen blur at sigma 2 to 8 is still 2 to 3 times slower. Before this: sigma 32 was 7.4 ms and sigma 16 3.9 ms on the whole layer. Layers are still drawn into a full-size content texture, allocated per layer per frame; a tight content texture and pooling it is the next saving. Mobile not measured.","files":null}]}